{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of each lottery based on the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Skewness here is defined in terms of whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (right-skewed). For Lottery A: 50% probability on 1000 Euros (favorable) and 50% on 0 Euros (unfavorable). For Lottery B: 100% on 500 Euros (a moderate, certain outcome). The distribution in Lottery A has equal probability on two extremes, one highly favorable and one highly unfavorable, while Lottery B has all probability on a single, intermediate outcome. A right-skewed distribution would typically have a long tail on the right (high values), but in this context, the definition is inverted: right-skewed means more probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Given that Lottery A assigns 50% to 0 (unfavorable) and 50% to 1000 (favorable), and Lottery B assigns 100% to 500 (neither clearly favorable nor unfavorable), the definition implies that a lottery is right-skewed if more probability is on unfavorable outcomes. However, in Lottery A, half the probability is on unfavorable (0), and half on favorable (1000). In Lottery B, there is no unfavorable outcome—500 is not unfavorable compared to 1000. Thus, Lottery A has a greater portion of probability on an unfavorable outcome (0) than Lottery B, which has no unfavorable outcome. Therefore, Lottery A is more right-skewed under this definition. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, so it would prefer Lottery A.",
