  "step_3": "First, the information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) involves comparing the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. Lottery B is right-skewed (more probability on the unfavorable outcome), while Lottery A has no skewness. The individual did not consider skewness or any aspect of the distribution’s shape. Second, the individual’s justification is based on 'hope for the win'—a desire for the favorable outcome (0 Euros) in Lottery B—rather than any evaluation of the distribution’s skewness. The reasoning is not aligned with the rule of preferring the more right-skewed lottery. The individual’s deliberation is driven by hope and subjective preference for a chance at a better outcome, not by the structural property of skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. However, the individual did express a preference for Lottery B (implied by the hope), which coincidentally aligns with the reason’s preference, but the justification is not based on the reason’s logic. Thus, the reason was not used.",
